# Handler boundaries

- Command and query handlers must never inject, resolve, or use Mediator dispatch interfaces, including `IMediator` and `ISender`. They must never invoke another command or query through direct handler calls or an intermediate component.
- Reuse domain methods and focused application components within the operation. Stage its changes and Outbox entries in the same DbContext and save once.
- Dispatch commands from entry points such as gRPC adapters and Inbox consumers. For an asynchronous follow-up, publish an integration event through the Outbox and handle it through the Inbox.

The project supplies the tracked document and an Outbox writer backed by the handler's DbContext. This operation persists the approval and the event together and returns the document ID.

```csharp
public async ValueTask<ApproveDocumentResponse> Handle(ApproveDocument command, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    var document = await context.Documents.SingleAsync(item => item.Id == command.Id, cancellationToken);
    document.Approve();
    await events.EnqueueApproved(document.Id, cancellationToken);

    await context.SaveChangesAsync(cancellationToken);
    return new ApproveDocumentResponse { Id = document.Id.ToString() };
}
```
